---
import OffClockPage from '../../components/OffClockPage.astro';
import { smallFavoritesSection } from '../../data/off-the-clock';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
---

<OffClockPage heading={smallFavoritesSection.title} description={smallFavoritesSection.description} noIndex>
  <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="ganeshas-heading">
    <h2 id="ganeshas-heading">Ganeshas of Mumbai</h2>
    <div class="bio">
      <p>
        My cousin in Mumbai got me curious about the history behind the city's Ganesh idols.
        He'd already found out quite a bit about some of them, and I wanted to see what else I could find.
        That's how Ganeshas of Mumbai started. I'm bringing together old photographs and documents to trace
        the histories of festival idols and temple shrines across the city. I'm still digging, and I'll share
        the archive here when it's ready.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every Ganesh Chaturthi, my cousin brings home a Ganesh idol. He uses the occasion to raise money,
        usually for an education project. That might mean a lab or toilets for a rural school, or help with school fees and supplies.
        We usually chip in a little.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you're in Mumbai during Ganeshotsav, you're welcome to join us at home and see how we celebrate.
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>Drop me a line</a> if you'd like to come by, hear more, or chip in.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="dosa-khakhra-heading">
    <h2 id="dosa-khakhra-heading">Dosa khakhra</h2>
    <p class="bio">
      One thing I always bring back from Mumbai is dosa khakhra from
      <a href="https://neelamfoodlandmumbai.com/collections/dosa-khakhra">Neelam Foodland</a>,
      a farsan shop in Khar full of snacks and sweets. I get a funny look when I say 'dosa khakhra' to someone
      who's never heard of it. A bite usually clears things up.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="entries" aria-labelledby="ratlam-heading">
    <h2 id="ratlam-heading">What I bring back from Ratlam</h2>
    <div class="bio">
      <p>
        My mom's family is from Ratlam, and I always bring back a packet of Ratlami sev when I visit.
        It's a crunchy, spicy snack, and I'm pretty particular about where I get mine. Ratlam, every time.
        Most namkeen shops in town will do fine. Ghasiram is a good place to start.
      </p>
      <p>
        I always get potato chips, too. I go to Ghasiram for the straightforward spicy ones and to
        Sanwariya Namkeen for the hing chips when I want that strong hit of asafoetida.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</OffClockPage>
